course: dictionary
lesson: 5882
title: Mankind and all people
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Mankind is a noun. It means all people in the world, thought of together as one group: the human race. «Armstrong said: That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.» Here mankind means every person on Earth, not just the men.
explain: Mankind is uncountable, so we do not say "a mankind" or "two mankinds". We talk about the history of mankind, or things that are for the good of mankind. To check your meaning, swap in humankind: if the sentence still makes sense, you have understood it.
explain: Be careful. Mankind has an older meaning, men as opposed to women, but this use is rare now. Many people prefer humankind or humanity, so that women are clearly included. When you read an old text, ask: does the sentence mean all people, or only men?
fact: Mankind means all people in the world, the human race.
fact: Mankind is uncountable: you say the history of mankind, not a mankind.
fact: Mankind can also mean men, as opposed to women, but this older use is rare now.
fact: Humankind and humanity are often used today so that women are clearly included.
fact: In 1969 Neil Armstrong said on the Moon: «That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.»
exercise: «Armstrong said: one giant leap for mankind.» Who does mankind include here? | Only men | *Every person on Earth, women and men | Only scientists | Only the astronauts on the Moon
exercise: «The history of mankind is full of inventions.» What does mankind mean here? | Only the men in history | *The human race | The Moon | A kind of machine
exercise: Which word could replace mankind in a modern sentence so that women are clearly included? | manly | *humanity | mankinds | a mankind
test: «The new medicine was for the good of mankind.» What is mankind in this sentence? | A kind of medicine | Only the men | *All people in the world | A small step
test: Which statement about mankind is true? | It can be counted: a mankind, two mankinds | It means only the Moon | It is a verb meaning to build | *It is uncountable, so we say the history of mankind
exam: In «the history of mankind», which meaning fits best? | *All people in the world, the human race | A type of tool | Mankind is a verb meaning to build | Only men
